1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,400
 So now let's talk about RAG and knowledge bases.

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:08,720
 So what is RAG? RAG is retrieval- Augmented generation.

3
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:12,960
 Behind this very fancy name, there is a very simple concept.

4
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:21,280
 This allows your Foundation model to reference a data source from outside of its training data without being fine-tuned.

5
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:22,600
 So how does that work?

6
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:27,560
 So we have a knowledge base and it's being built and managed by Amazon Bedrock.

7
00:00:27,560 --> 00:00:30,200
 For this, it must rely on a data source.

8
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:32,200
 For example, Amazon is free.

9
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:34,640
 So your data is going to be an Amazon is free.

10
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:37,000
 And then automatically Bedrock is going to do some magic.

11
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:40,720
 We'll see how that works exactly, but it's going to build a knowledge base.

12
00:00:40,720 --> 00:00:45,200
 And then a user is going to ask a question to your Foundation model,

13
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:47,880
 saying who is the product manager for John.

14
00:00:47,880 --> 00:00:50,360
 So that's probably something related to his company.

15
00:00:50,360 --> 00:00:53,760
 And of course, the Foundation model does not know anything about John,

16
00:00:53,760 --> 00:00:58,920
 because John is a very specific query for my company and my own data.

17
00:00:58,920 --> 00:01:02,560
 So there's going to be something called a search.

18
00:01:02,560 --> 00:01:07,120
 And this information is going to be searched in the knowledge base.

19
00:01:07,120 --> 00:01:10,320
 Automatically, of course, this is all happening behind the scene.

20
00:01:10,320 --> 00:01:15,200
 But this knowledge base is backed by something called a vector database.

21
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:20,560
 And to create the data in the vector database, again, Bedrock takes care of everything.

22
00:01:20,560 --> 00:01:22,920
 It's called creating vector embeddings.

23
00:01:22,920 --> 00:01:24,960
 And I will show you how that works later on.

24
00:01:24,960 --> 00:01:28,280
 But basically, thanks to this vector database,

25
00:01:28,280 --> 00:01:33,840
 then we're able to retrieve the relevant information out of this knowledge base.

26
00:01:33,840 --> 00:01:38,320
 And for example, we get some text back from this, saying that we have some information

27
00:01:38,320 --> 00:01:41,440
 about John, such as we have some support contacts.

28
00:01:41,440 --> 00:01:44,040
 We have a product manager, which is Jesse Smith.

29
00:01:44,040 --> 00:01:47,040
 We have an engineer, which is Sarah Ronald.

30
00:01:47,040 --> 00:01:51,560
 And so all this is going to go as an augmented prompt.

31
00:01:51,560 --> 00:01:57,160
 And so it's going to be the original query, as well as the text that has been retrieved.

32
00:01:57,160 --> 00:02:02,120
 They're going to be passed together into the actual foundation model.

33
00:02:02,120 --> 00:02:06,160
 And the foundation model is going to look at this augmented prompt.

34
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:12,480
 And generate a response saying, hey, it looks like Jesse Smith is the product manager for John.

35
00:02:12,480 --> 00:02:15,400
 And it's what it's called retrieval augmented generation.

36
00:02:15,400 --> 00:02:20,920
 Retrieval because we retrieve the data outside of the foundation model.

37
00:02:20,920 --> 00:02:26,040
 And it's augmented generation because we augment the prompt with that external data

38
00:02:26,040 --> 00:02:27,040
 that has been retrieved.

39
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:28,800
 So hence the name RAG.

40
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:34,800
 And so RAG in AWS Amazon bedrock is going to be a knowledge base.

41
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:40,680
 So this is very helpful when you need to have data that needs to be fed.

42
00:02:40,680 --> 00:02:43,520
 And that is very up to date that is in real time.

43
00:02:43,520 --> 00:02:46,160
 And it needs to be fed into the foundation model.

44
00:02:46,160 --> 00:02:47,280
 So how does that work?

45
00:02:47,280 --> 00:02:52,520
 Well, here we have an example, give me talking points for benefits of air travel.

46
00:02:52,520 --> 00:02:56,800
 As you can see, one of these talking points has a little one on it.

47
00:02:56,800 --> 00:03:01,520
 And a link to air travel that PDF and that PDF maybe in Amazon history.

48
00:03:01,520 --> 00:03:06,560
 So this is an example where the RAG and the knowledge base has been used to

49
00:03:06,560 --> 00:03:10,120
 give an answer to a specific prompt.

50
00:03:10,120 --> 00:03:13,960
 So everything is to go into a vector database.

51
00:03:13,960 --> 00:03:15,200
 And so we have several options.

52
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:20,600
 So the vector databases on AWS and Amazon bedrock can be of several kinds.

53
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:27,040
 So we have two services on AWS that are possible to use as your vector database.

54
00:03:27,040 --> 00:03:30,280
 The first one is called open search service.

55
00:03:30,280 --> 00:03:33,040
 And the second one is called Amazon Aurora.

56
00:03:33,040 --> 00:03:38,000
 So remember the names Amazon open search service or Amazon Aurora.

57
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:41,360
 And we have three other options and maybe there'll be more in the future of course, but

58
00:03:41,360 --> 00:03:44,960
 we have three other options right now to use for a vector database.

59
00:03:44,960 --> 00:03:48,560
 We have MongoDB, Redis or PineCon.

60
00:03:48,560 --> 00:03:53,960
 And so you can configure an knowledge database backed by any kind of these vector databases.

61
00:03:53,960 --> 00:03:58,560
 But if you don't specify anything, AWS is going to create for you and

62
00:03:58,560 --> 00:04:01,960
 open search service serverless database for you.

63
00:04:01,960 --> 00:04:05,440
 So next we need to have a golden embedding model.

64
00:04:05,440 --> 00:04:08,960
 And this is how to convert the data into these vectors.

65
00:04:08,960 --> 00:04:13,000
 And so it could be Amazon Titan or cohere.

66
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:17,240
 And it doesn't matter if you don't use these models as your foundation model,

67
00:04:17,240 --> 00:04:20,640
 the embeddings model and the foundation model can be different.

68
00:04:20,640 --> 00:04:25,080
 So your S3 documents are going to be chunked.

69
00:04:25,080 --> 00:04:27,920
 And that means they're going to be split into different parts.

70
00:04:27,920 --> 00:04:31,280
 And these parts are going to be fed into the embeddings model,

71
00:04:31,280 --> 00:04:36,640
 which is going to generate vectors and place these vector in the vector database.

72
00:04:36,640 --> 00:04:40,600
 And the outcome is that now these vectors are easily searchable.

73
00:04:40,600 --> 00:04:43,280
 And so when RAG will look into the vector database,

74
00:04:43,280 --> 00:04:48,560
 it will find the right passage in the right document to be able to augment the query.

75
00:04:48,560 --> 00:04:52,960
 So the exam may ask you to choose the right RAG vector database,

76
00:04:52,960 --> 00:04:54,280
 at least from a high level.

77
00:04:54,280 --> 00:04:55,920
 So you're going to be expert at databases.

78
00:04:55,920 --> 00:04:59,240
 I'm going to give you more information that you need to figure out database.

79
00:04:59,240 --> 00:05:03,880
 But the first categories I would say would be Amazon Open Search Service and

80
00:05:03,880 --> 00:05:05,560
 Amazon DocumentDB.

81
00:05:05,560 --> 00:05:09,760
 So Open Search Service is a search and analytics database.

82
00:05:09,760 --> 00:05:13,360
 And it's very good is going to be definitely the preferred choices.

83
00:05:13,360 --> 00:05:15,560
 And you're going to have real-time similarity queries and

84
00:05:15,560 --> 00:05:18,280
 you can store millions of vector embeddings.

85
00:05:18,280 --> 00:05:20,800
 The reason it's so good, and that could be an exam question,

86
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:24,600
 is that you have a very good scalable index management.

87
00:05:24,600 --> 00:05:28,800
 And also you have very, very fast nearest neighbor search capability,

88
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:30,600
 also called KNN.

89
00:05:30,600 --> 00:05:35,440
 The other one that's really high performance is Amazon DocumentDB,

90
00:05:35,440 --> 00:05:38,960
 with MongoDB compatibility, which is a NoSQL database.

91
00:05:38,960 --> 00:05:41,600
 And again, you get the real-time similarity queries and

92
00:05:41,600 --> 00:05:44,000
 store millions of vector embeddings.

93
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,840
 So I would keep these ones together.

94
00:05:46,840 --> 00:05:51,320
 Then we have Amazon Aurora and Amazon RDS for Postgres SQL.

95
00:05:51,320 --> 00:05:55,440
 So both of them are going to be relational databases.

96
00:05:55,440 --> 00:05:59,720
 Aurora is proprietary on AWS, but it's cloud friendly.

97
00:05:59,720 --> 00:06:03,120
 And RDS for Postgres SQL is original database as well.

98
00:06:03,120 --> 00:06:06,680
 But it's open source, both are fine.

99
00:06:06,680 --> 00:06:09,720
 Usually, if you see relational database, think one of these two.

100
00:06:09,720 --> 00:06:12,560
 And finally, if you see graph database, choose Neptune.

101
00:06:12,560 --> 00:06:14,880
 So I don't think there's going to be any question asking you to choose between

102
00:06:14,880 --> 00:06:17,560
 the five databases together.

103
00:06:17,560 --> 00:06:22,520
 But definitely, you'll see a question about choosing our relational database

104
00:06:22,520 --> 00:06:24,080
 for a vector database.

105
00:06:24,080 --> 00:06:27,040
 And you may have to choose Aurora or RDS for Postgres SQL.

106
00:06:27,040 --> 00:06:31,080
 Or if you see something around high performance and real-time similarity queries and

107
00:06:31,080 --> 00:06:33,840
 millions of vectors embeddings, is going to be open source surveys or

108
00:06:33,840 --> 00:06:36,240
 DocumentDB or you see graph Neptune.

109
00:06:36,240 --> 00:06:36,880
 That's about it.

110
00:06:36,880 --> 00:06:39,280
 I don't want to go too deep into these databases.

111
00:06:39,280 --> 00:06:43,280
 But this should be enough for the exam for you to get going.

112
00:06:43,280 --> 00:06:47,600
 So what kind of data sources can we use in Amazon Bedrock?

113
00:06:47,600 --> 00:06:51,480
 Well, we have Amazon S3, which is a place where you can put a lot of files in

114
00:06:51,480 --> 00:06:52,840
 the cloud directory.

115
00:06:52,840 --> 00:06:57,840
 Confluence, Microsoft SharePoint, Salesforce, as well as web pages.

116
00:06:57,840 --> 00:07:01,080
 So it could be your website, it could be your social media feed, et cetera.

117
00:07:01,080 --> 00:07:02,840
 So anything in the web.

118
00:07:02,840 --> 00:07:07,800
 Again, I'm pretty sure that Amazon Bedrock will add more sources over time.

119
00:07:07,800 --> 00:07:12,440
 But from an exam perspective, I think remembering Amazon S3, and maybe these

120
00:07:12,440 --> 00:07:13,520
 ones should be enough.

121
00:07:13,520 --> 00:07:17,520
 And of course, if from an exam perspective, you need to know about more data

122
00:07:17,520 --> 00:07:18,520
 sources.

123
00:07:18,520 --> 00:07:21,240
 I will of course include it in this slide.

124
00:07:21,240 --> 00:07:24,400
 So what are the use cases now for Amazon Bedrock?

125
00:07:24,400 --> 00:07:28,120
 Well, you can build a customer service chatbot where the knowledge base is going to be

126
00:07:28,120 --> 00:07:31,920
 your products, your feature, your specifications, troubleshooting guides, and

127
00:07:31,920 --> 00:07:33,720
 frequently asked questions.

128
00:07:33,720 --> 00:07:38,280
 And so therefore your rag application can be the chatbot that will answer customer queries

129
00:07:38,280 --> 00:07:40,760
 and look up in this knowledge base.

130
00:07:40,760 --> 00:07:44,920
 It could be for legal research analysis, where the knowledge base is going to be laws

131
00:07:44,920 --> 00:07:49,840
 and regulations, case precedents, legal opinions and expert analysis.

132
00:07:49,840 --> 00:07:54,160
 And this time we can have a chatbot that is going to have relevant information anytime

133
00:07:54,160 --> 00:07:56,880
 we have a specific legal query.

134
00:07:56,880 --> 00:07:59,000
 And we can have also a health care question answering.

135
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:03,840
 So again, the knowledge base can be diseases, treatments, clinical guidelines, research papers

136
00:08:03,840 --> 00:08:06,040
 and previous patient data.

137
00:08:06,040 --> 00:08:11,400
 And the application could be a chatbot that will answer complex medical queries.

138
00:08:11,400 --> 00:08:16,880
 So rag opens up a lot of possibilities for doing JNAI on AWS.

139
00:08:16,880 --> 00:08:18,280
 So I hope you liked it.

140
00:08:18,280 --> 00:08:19,920
 And I will see you in the next lecture.

